From e38a6fc6a78cac78632caed42e0bed059442a2f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vihan Aggarwal <mooimacow1234567@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 17:19:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] Spawn the canonical-mask builder at the clock's start
 instead of forking the trainer

Record #350 forks rank 0 at t0, i.e. forks the whole warmed-up 8-GPU trainer: fork() copies its
page tables, and every page the trainer writes in the first steps takes a copy-on-write fault
while the child lives, all on rank 0's launch path, which every rank then waits for at its next
collective. The fork came in with the canonical masking (#350) in the track_1_short refactor
(f380c1f), which measured 40.90 s against 40.60 s for #360 on the same nodes.

The builder is now a fresh interpreter, spawned (vfork + exec) at t0: python -I
track_1_short/canonical_mask_build.py FD VOCAB, writing into a memfd that the trainer maps and
page-locks before the clock, as it page-locked the shared buffer before. The build code moved
verbatim into that torch-free module. The child's startup, imports, tokenizer load and build all
run on the clock (more than before, where the fork inherited the loaded tokenizer), off the
training loop's path. start() costs 0.5 ms against 8.7 ms for os.fork() of a process with 2 GB
touched (CPU), before any copy-on-write fault.

Systems-only: the mask is byte-identical (tools/tests/test_canonical_mask_spawn.py).

